PRIVATE REFLECTION: {
Round 1 split is 12 ULDL vs 6 DLDLUL, with no new alternatives. ULDL remains majority and my current best: it avoids entering the dragon’s chamber entirely and never approaches D, !, or ?; the only cost is using the unlit # upper/central corridor, but # is not listed as a hazard. Valid route: @ row4 col26 → U to row2 col26 → L along # to col15 → D to row4 col15 → L through row4 col8 door into gold room at g row4 col5.

DLDLUL is valid but riskier under explicit-hazard reading: @ → D through row5 col26 door into right lit room → L to col24 → D to row11 col24 (left of dragon) → L through row11 col23 door/central # to col16 → U to row4 col16 → L through row4 col8 door to gold. It does not step on D row11 col26, potion row7 col25, or scroll row13 col25, but it enters the dragon’s chamber and passes near those hazard tiles.

Main unresolved dispute: whether a long unlit # corridor is itself dangerous. DLDLUL supporters argue lit visibility and avoiding prolonged # exposure make it safer; ULDL supporters argue # is not marked dangerous, while D is, and DLDLUL unnecessarily enters D’s room. I still favor ULDL because “least dangerous” should prioritize avoiding explicit hazards and adjacency to the dragon/potion/scroll; DLDLUL is a plausible alternative only if unlit tiles are treated as hazardous. Invalid/weak: L, RULDL, LULDL, because immediate L/R from @ are void or routes add unnecessary detours. Key map anchors: @ row4 col26; gold row4 col5; dragon row11 col26; potion row7 col25; scroll row13 col25; doors row4 col8, row5 col26, row11 col23. Watch future rounds for whether unlit # or dragon adjacency is considered a real danger.
}

